summary: Major bug in First Encounter makes it unplayable.

I have recently purchased First Encounter (Elite 3, I suppose it can be
called). As well as the minor bugs, i.e.

1.	Cannot use joystick, as it cannot be calibrated within the program,
	even with joystick calibration wheels (I couldn't find any calib-
	ration command info in the program itself or in the manual).

2.	Every half hour or so, crashing the computer with an unhandled
	exception error (VERY VERY ANNOYING!).

3.	Music notes 'hanging' all the time when using an AWE32 (making
	music unlistenable after a short while).

there also exists a MAJOR bug. I would have laughed so much if it wasn't
such a fundemental error that it insults game purchasers so much. Clearly
there has been little, IF ANY, testing of the finished game.

To introduce this bug, I shall go back to the old days of my Atari ST,
which I had before purchasing my 486 PC. The game, Frontier - Elite II,
came out, and was hyped up for the ST market (which was a dying market,
now pretty much dead). I liked this game, which was lacking in a few areas
but quite good for the old ST. Anyway, there was this bug, which I found
after a lot of gameplay, which effectively crashed the machine when you
tried to purchase a ship which was smaller than your current one. This
meant that you couldn't do some of the 'side-missions' such as taking on 
contract killings of other pilots or passengers, or fast delivery of
goods or persons, which required small-ish, agile and fast craft, if you
had already progressed to be a trader with a large ship.

Anyway, guess what, this bug has been fixed in First Encounters. But guess
what they have replaced it with. This program crashes when you try and buy
a ship which is BIGGER than the one you have got. Considering you start
with a measly small ship and are supposed, yea encouraged, to upgrade as
soon as you can to a larger ship, of which there are many, means that this
bug effectively kills the game flat right near the start.

I would hazard a guess that this game has hardly been tested, certainly not
tested to any acceptable standard for the PC market.

Anyway, I did what I suppose a lot of people have done and will do, which
is took the game back to the shop I bought it from and demanded my money back.
Since the shop was GAME, they gave me my money back straight away, no bother.
GAME are good like that. Other retailers may not be so understandable.

All I can say to David Braben, and Gametek, and anyone involved in the manage-
ment of this game, is:

You should be very ashamed of yourselves. You failed big this time. A lot of
people who, like me, enjoyed the old Elite and even gave Elite II a chance
have been ripped off big time by you lot. Don't rip us off anymore. Oh,
and if you're thinking of another sequel, don't bother. Your reputation has
rightly been squashed underfoot, where it belongs. No excuses or game fixes
will help you. I would encourage you to diversify into another realm of
employment, e.g. basket weaving. So there!

Anyway, nastiness over, this game really is full of bugs, at least one
major fundamental one, and if other companies can produce games without
these bugs, there is really no excuse for David Braben and Gametek.

Oh, yes, and if anyone is thinking of buying it, you know the score. Don't
bother.

'Nuff Said.
